Consider the solar wind modeled as a magnetized plasma flowing radially outward from the Sun through the heliosphere. Within this flow, transverse magnetic and velocity fluctuations satisfying the Alfvén relation (correlated b and v perturbations propagating along the mean magnetic field at the Alfvén speed) are observed in the inner heliosphere to be predominantly outward-propagating with high normalized cross helicity. As heliocentric distance increases, these outward-propagating Alfvénic fluctuations are observed to lose their Alfvénic character (normalized cross helicity decreases, residual energy becomes more negative, and the magnetic-to-kinetic energy ratio departs from unity) and eventually to be replaced by more isotropic, fully developed MHD turbulence in the outer heliosphere.

Within the framework of compressible MHD describing such a heliospheric plasma, provide a mechanistic explanation of the radial evolution and eventual disappearance of the outward-propagating Alfvénic fluctuations. In particular, identify and quantify the roles played by:
(i) large-scale velocity shear between fast and slow solar wind streams (including shear-driven nonlinear couplings and Kelvin–Helmholtz-type instabilities beyond the Alfvén critical zone);
(ii) stream–stream interaction regions where fast wind overtakes slow wind, producing compressions and rarefactions that stir the fluctuations;
(iii) proximity to the heliospheric current sheet, where the radial mean magnetic field weakens and transverse gradients in the background field become important.

Explain how these three mechanisms together drive the cascade and decorrelation that transform a population of outward-propagating Alfvén waves emitted from the corona into the observed turbulent state of the outer heliosphere.

